 { Again White's pawn majority is clearly the more mobile one, due to Black's doubled f-pawns. Fischer proceeded with precision. } 1. { -KEY- } c5 $1 ( { The c-pawn should move first, since } 1. d5 $6 c5 $1 { would let Black blockade the d-pawn with improved defensive prospects. If now } 2. Nf2 { heading for e4, then simply } f5 ) 
Bb8 2. d5 cxd5 $6 { Too co-operative. } ( { a) } 2... Re2 3. Kd3 Rxg2 4. d6 $1 { with ideas like Ne5-d7 and Re1-e8, clearly favours White; } ) 
 ( { b) but } 2... f5 $1 { seems stronger, avoiding the knight check on e5 that follows in the game; e.g. } 3. dxc6 bxc6 $1 4. Nf2 Nf6 5. Bxf6 Kxf6 6. Nd3 Bc7 { with far better chances of resistance. } ) 
3. Rxd5 f5 4. Ne5+ Bxe5 5. Rxe5 $1 ( { After } 5. Bxe5 $6 Nf6 $1 6. Bxf6 Kxf6 { Black achieves the defender's preferred type of ending - a rook ending - and the win becomes more difficult in practice. } ) 
Nf6 ( { Avoiding the rook exchange would let White seize the seventh rank with 6.Re7, while } 5... Rxe5 6. Bxe5 { leaves the knight marginalized from the battle, just like in the game. At this point, } Nf6 { runs into } ( { or similarly } 6... Ng7 7. Bxg7 { etc. } ) 
7. Bxf6 $1 Kxf6 8. Kd3 { and White wins comfortably. } ) 
6. Rxe8 Nxe8 7. Be5 $1 { A final accuracy which leaves the knight immobile, since if it goes to f6 or g7 White will again just swap it off and then use his pawn majority to win the pawn ending. } Kh5 ( { This counterattack is bound to fail; but } 7... Kf7 { was equally hopeless after } 8. Kd3 Ke6 9. Kd4 { followed by b2-b4, a2-a4 etc. } ) 
8. Kd3 g4 9. b4 $1 { Fischer isn't distracted by the f4-pawn, preferring to advance his pawn majority, aware that he is practically a piece up. } a6 10. a4 gxf3 11. gxf3 Kh4 12. b5 axb5 13. a5 $5 Kh3 14. c6 
 1-0

