 { Here is an excerpt from my own practice. Despite White's space advantage Black is better in the above position, thanks to his sounder structure - or more mobile pawn majority, if you prefer. With his next move Waitzkin takes the first step to the realization of this advantage. } 1... { -KEY- } b6 $1 { Preparing ...Kb7, ...a7-a6 and ...b6-b5, with a slow but firm advance of the pawn majority. } 2. Ke2 Kb7 3. Rhd1 $2 { This is very bad - and just the kind of move that, many years later, I am trying to get my students to avoid in their games. When you have a poor structure, don't exchange too many pieces (in particular, the rooks); instead, look for dynamics and activity. } ( 3. Rhf1 $1 { was correct, preparing f4-f5 with some counterplay on the f-file. Obviously, } g6 { is just met by } 4. g4 { , insisting on the plan. } ) 
a6 4. Be1 Rxd1 5. Rxd1 Rxd1 6. Kxd1 b5 7. axb5 axb5 8. Nd2 ( { Or } 8. Nd6 Bxd6 9. exd6 Kc8 10. e4 e5 { and the d6-pawn will fall sooner or later. } ) 
Na5 9. b3 Kc6 10. Kc2 $6 ( { I should rather have played } 10. Ke2 c4 11. bxc4 Nxc4 12. Nb3 $1 ) 
c4 $1 { Finally, the pawn majority is converted into a passed pawn, whereas White's extra pawn on the kingside has no real significance. } 11. bxc4 Nxc4 { As we just discussed, exchanges usually favour the side with the superior pawn structure. } 12. Nxc4 bxc4 13. e4 Kb5 14. g4 Bc5 15. Bc3 Bf2 16. f5 Kc5 $1 { With the threat of ...Bd4. In hindsight, the fact that White has a pawn fixed on the same colour as his bishop plays heavily against him in this endgame. Remember that with rooks on the board (cf. 3.Rhf1!), there is no such 'colour problem'! } 17. fxe6 fxe6 18. Bb2 Be1 19. h3 Bf2 20. Kd2 Kb4 21. Kc2 Kc5 22. Kd2 Kb4 23. Kc2 Be1 $1 { Preparing a deadly ...c4-c3. } 24. Bd4 c3 25. Bb6 Kc4 26. Ba7 Bg3 27. Bb8 ( { This lets Black's king invade decisively. A waiting move like } 27. Be3 { was preferable, since after } Bxe5 28. Bf2 { , the white bishop might actually be able to keep away Black's king, e.g. } Bd4 29. Bg3 Be3 30. Be5 $1 { etc. } ) 
Kd4 { The rest is easy - Black wins in outside passed pawn fashion. } 28. Ba7+ Kxe4 29. Kxc3 Bxe5+ 30. Kd2 Kf3 $1 31. Kd3 Bf4 { In view of the inevitable ...e6-e5-e4-e3, White resigned. } 
 0-1

