 { Here is a final case featuring a 'defective' pawn majority on one of the sides. White is far better thanks to his more active rook and favourable pawn structure - on the queenside his two pawns stop three, while on the opposite flank there should, sooner or later, emerge a white passed pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } Kf4 $1 { A nice piece of provocation - after ...e6-e5 Black's sixth rank will prove severely weakened; besides, the plan of creating a passed pawn is facilitated. } e5+ ( { Passive defence won't help either: } 1... Rc7 2. e5 Kg7 3. Kg5 Re7 4. g4 $1 { , creating an outside passed pawn on the h-file with an easy win. } ) 
2. Ke3 Kg7 3. g3 $1 { The virtue of 1 Kf4 becomes evident: Black can't prevent f3-f4. } Re7 4. f4 exf4+ 5. gxf4 Kf6 6. Rd6+ Kg7 7. e5 Kf7 8. Ke4 Kg7 9. f5 $1 { Thus White reaches f5 with his king, which means that e5-e6 can be effected without worrying about ...Kf6. } gxf5+ 10. Kxf5 ( { With the simple plan of e5-e6, followed by Rd7; and } 10. Kxf5 Rf7 11. Ke6 { leaves Black helpless against the threat of } 12. Rd7 { , so he resigned. } ) 

 1-0

