 { Next, let's see two examples originating from 'Mastering Chess Strategy', where both sides have sound pawn majorities.The queenside pawn majority is a common feature in some openings, such as the c2-c3 Sicilian, the French Tarrasch with 3...c5 4 exd5 Qxd5, and others. In the structure above, Black is usually reluctant to swap too many pieces, since an advance of his central majority tends to be a bit slower than White's corresponding play on the queenside. Thus the above position is a kind of dream scenario for White - and he has even managed to put his king in an ideal place for the looming queenside expansion. } 1. { -KEY- } b5 $1 { Erenburg advances his pawn majority and at the same time restricts the enemy bishop. The fact that he fixes his pawns on squares of the 'wrong' colour and also cedes the c5-square isn't a big deal, since Black lacks the space to exploit this. } ( { a) In contrast, both } 1. c5 $6 Bc6 2. f3 Nd5 3. b5 Nxc3 4. Kxc3 Bd5 ) 
 ( { b) and } 1. Bf3 Bc6 $1 2. Bxc6 bxc6 { lead to a lesser advantage than in the game. } ) 
b6 $2 { This natural move converts that pawn into a weakness. } ( 1... Rd8 2. Bf3 Bc8 { was correct, when } 3. Rxd8 Kxd8 4. c5 { yields a clear, but not decisive advantage - Erenburg. } ) 
2. Bf3 Rb8 ( { After } 2... Ra7 $2 3. Na4 { the problem with ...b7-b6 is well displayed. Thus Black has to surrender the a-file. } ) 
3. Ra1 $1 { This move comes with two strong ideas: to seize the seventh rank by Ra7 or attack the b6-pawn by Ra6 and Na4. } Bc8 { Preparing ...Nd7-c5. } 4. Ra7+ Nd7 5. Bc6 $1 { Now White is ready to exchange the defending knight at any moment. } f5 ( { The immediate } 5... Kd6 { fails to } 6. Ne4 { so Black prepares it. } ) 
6. Na4 Kd6 7. Bxd7 $1 { Just at the right moment, before the knight escapes to e5. } Bxd7 8. Kb4 $1 { Active king. } ( { After } 8. Kb4 { , the threat of }  9. Nxb6 $1 Rxb6 10. c5 { forces the black king to move away from the b6-pawn, and the rest is easy. } ) 
Ke7 9. Ra6 e5 10. Rxb6 Rd8 11. Rb7 Kf6 12. Nc5 Be8 13. Rc7 { In view of b5-b6-b7 next, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

