 { White is better here, mainly thanks to his bishop pair, an asset which tends to become more powerful the further the game goes on. Note that the light-squared bishop is doing a good job in restricting Black's desired plan of ...a7-a6 and ...b6-b5. Bauer found a clever way of improving his central pawn majority. } 1. { -KEY- } g4 $1 { Clearing the way for the e-pawn. By the way, this move would have been just as strong in the event of a normal black structure with pawns on f5-g6-h7. } ( { Less constructive is } 1. e4 $6 Bxf4 2. exf5 gxf5 3. Bxf5 Nd4 ) 
fxg4 $6 { This converts the e-pawn into a strong passed pawn. } ( 1... Ne7 { was more stubborn, although after } 2. Kf2 Kf7 3. Kf3 { , it is not easy to suggest a plan for Black, whereas White could contemplate e3-e4 at a suitable moment. } ) 
2. hxg4 g5 3. Be4 $1 ( { Black intended } 3. fxg5 $2 Be5 { with a blockade on the dark squares, but White is not distracted from his original plan. } ) 
Nd8 4. f5 { With the strong idea of Bd5 and e3-e4-e5. } Nf7 5. Bd5 Kf8 6. Bxf7 $1 { Eliminating the idea of a blockade on e5. } Kxf7 7. Kf2 g6 8. Kf3 { Of course the pawns should remain connected. The rest is relatively easy, with the e-pawn asking to be pushed. } gxf5 9. gxf5 a6 10. e4 b5 11. axb5 axb5 12. e5 b4 13. e6+ Ke7 14. Bd2 Be5 ( { Or } 14... Kf6 15. Bxg5 $1 { - Deviatkin. } ) 
15. Bxg5+ Ke8 16. f6 Bxb2 17. f7+ Kf8 18. Ke4 b3 19. Kf5 Bg7 20. Kg6 
 1-0

